v4.0.35
Radon Player 4.0.35: sync makes both devices' songs the same, a song's file is never changed while it plays, and the rest of an album to stream
**Install or update:** `RadonPlayer-Next.apk` (Android), `Radon-Player-4.0.35.msi` (Windows), `radon-player_4.0.35_amd64.deb` (Linux). Each installs over the version you have and keeps your settings and stats.

**What is new**
- **What damaged the files, found:** damaged songs taken from a phone and compared with good copies showed each to be a patchwork of two versions of itself, 4,000 bytes apart: two tag writes at work on the same file at once, one of them moving the music along inside it. Since 4.0.32 only one write to a song file happens at a time, and each is made on a copy that then replaces the file in one pass; this release adds the two guards below. Before it, the writers were run on copies of real songs 240 at a time, and the app was killed in the middle of writes 200 times: the music came back exactly as it was every time.
- **A song's file is never changed while it plays:** no scan, tagger or cover write touches the file of the song playing or the next one. A scan leaves it for another time. A change you make yourself is kept and goes into the file a moment after the song has finished (or when the app next starts); a note says it is waiting.
- **A song being written isn't played half-written:** a song whose file is being put down at the very moment it should play is given five seconds; if the write hasn't ended, the song is passed over and the next one plays, without asking. A note says why, and it is kept in Settings › Problems (nothing is wrong with the file).
- **Hear the rest of an album before you download it:** the songs in Missing from this album (or EP) are found on your streaming service and listed like any streamed song: tap one to play it, queue it, add it to a playlist, pick several. Beside Download missing tracks, **Listen later** puts them all aside.
- **Listen later: the album's songs I don't have:** with a song or an album picked anywhere, this new option (under Add to Listen later playlist) puts only the songs of its album you haven't got on Listen later. Add to Listen later playlist still takes every song picked.
- **Listen later › Review:** lists the songs on it you haven't got, each to download, leave as a streaming song, or remove. A small Discovery of one album.
- **The damaged list keeps up:** a song leaves it as soon as its new copy is downloaded, so what is left is what still needs replacing. **Check these again** reads only the listed files once more (seconds, not a full check): one that is whole now, because you put a good copy in its place yourself, leaves the list too.
- **Sync makes the other device's songs exactly the main device's (Tags match):** pick the main device, and at each sync the other device's copy of each song is made to say what the main device's says: title, artist, album, album artist, genre, year, track, the lyrics and their mood, the moods (in the file and in the app, extra ones taken off) and the cover. What differs is replaced and what the main device's copy hasn't got is taken off; what is the same already isn't written again.
- **A failsafe in that:** something is only taken off when the main device read its copy for certain and it truly has none. A file it couldn't read, or moods or a cover it couldn't tell, leaves the other device's as they are; a song whose name couldn't be read isn't changed at all, and a title or artist is never taken off.
- **One Sync does it all:** when there are more covers than fit in one go, the sync carries on by itself, part after part, until every one has gone across.
- **A cover you pick goes to your other device** at the next Sync, as your tag edits do.
- **Low song count albums** holds only the albums you have one to four songs of; five or more are in Albums.
- **Covers in Wrapped:** a single the app downloaded showed its artist's picture (or nothing) in place of its cover: its own file's cover is used now, and a name is also looked up without its featured artist. And any song, album, single or EP in Wrapped can have its cover picked by hand: right-click it (long-press on the phone) and choose one found on Google, Genius or Wikipedia, a picture of the artist, or a picture of yours. It is kept for that row from then on, and put into your own file of it when it is yours.
- **Play and pause from the desktop's media controls (Linux):** the app told the desktop "can't play, can't pause" for the moment it started with no song, and never said otherwise, so a media menu that went by that left the play button doing nothing (next and previous worked). It now says so whenever a song comes or goes.
